t range.\n\nPrimary output: normalized throughput-vs-m curves per method with confidence intervals, a bubble estimate per (method, m), and a pre-registered pass/fail decision rule for (O3).\n\n## 2) Factors and levels\n- Factor M (method): 4 levels {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}.\n- Factor m (number of microbatches per global step): geometric sweep m \u2208 {2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}, which already contains the boundary/reference values m = p (4) and m = 2p (8) at the primary p. If the scheduler enforces m \u2265 p for a given method (a possible hardening rule, esp. 1F1B), drop the illegal small-m levels for That Cell only, document the drop, and rely on m \u2208 {p, 2p, \u2026} for the trend \u2014 but every effort is made to keep m \u2208 {2, 4} for all methods, since the question hinges on small-m behavior (esp. O3).\n- Secondary factor p (pipeline stages): primary level p = 4 (full M \u00d7 m grid). Sensitivity subset at p = 8 (all four methods \u00d7 m \u2208 {4, 8, 16, 32}, 2 seeds) to confirm the pattern is not an artifact of one world size.\n- Fixed c (1F1B-I only): c = 2 chunks per stage, constant across its m sweep.\n- Global batch framing (locked primary): micro_batch is FIXED at 4 sequences; global_batch = m \u00d7 micro_batch = 4m. Justification: each microbatch is an identical, fixed-shape compute unit, so the only thing that changes along the m axis is the schedule\u2019s interleaving of identical units \u2014 precisely the axis that isolates scheduling/bubble effects. Throughput is token-normalized (section 3) so the growing per-step token count does not bias the comparison. The alternative (fixed global batch with per-microbatch batch = G/m shrinking as m grows) changes tensor shapes and hence kernel efficiency, confounding schedule effects, and is therefore relegated to a sensitivity note only (run at p=4, M \u2208 {1F1B, ZB-2p}, m \u2208 {4, 16, 64} if budget allows).\n\n## 3) Response variable and measurement protocol\nResponse: steady-state throughput TP(M, m) = (tokens covered in the measurement window) / (elapsed wall time of that window), in tokens/s. Tokens covered = N_measure \u00d7 global_batch \u00d7 seq_len = N_measure \u00d7 4m \u00d7 2048 (with the fixed micro_batch = 4, seq_len = 2048).\n\nPer-run protocol:\n1. Warm-up phase: run global steps until a stability criterion is met: the moving-median of per-step times over the latest 10 steps stays within \u00b15% of its own value for 10 consecutive steps, with a minimum warm-up of max(20, 5p) steps and a hard cap of 200 steps (if the cap is hit without stabilization, flag the run and retain it with an annotation; this cap is only a guard).\n2. Measurement window: exactly N_measure = 100 consecutive global steps immediately after warm-up, before any teardown. Per-step wall time {t_i} is recorded (torch.cuda.synchronize around each step). TP = (100 \u00d7 4m \u00d7 2048) / \u03a3 t_i.\n3. Cooldown is automatically excluded because measurement stops before drain; no drain steps are timed.\n4. One run = one (M, m, p, seed) cell; seeds set per rank via torch.manual_seed(seed) with all processes seeded identically (no convergence objective; seeds only control data/order for run-to-run variance realism). Pin each repeat to the same GPU type and clock settings; disable cuDNN autotune for determinism.\n\n## 4) Fixed controls (identical across every cell)\n- p: 4 (primary) or 8 (sensitivity subset); DDP/TP/DP all disabled so the only parallelism is pipeline.\n- Model: GPT-style decoder \u2014 num_layers = 32 (divisible by p and p\u00d7c: 8 layers/stage at p=4, 4 layers/stage at p=8; 4 layers/chunk for 1F1B-I at p=4, c=2), hidden_size = 1024, num_attention_heads = 16, head_dim 64, ffn_hidden = 4096, vocab_size \u2248 50k.\n- seq_len = 2048; micro_batch = 4 (fixed); global_batch = 4m.\n- Optimizer: Adam (\u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999, \u03b5=1e-8), fixed LR = 3e-4, no LR schedule; weight decay 0.1; gradient clipping 1.0. Same across cells so optimizer-side compute is constant (convergence is irrelevant to the throughput metric).\n- Precision: BF16 (with FP32 master weights), deterministic algorithms enabled; same NCCL backend and same intra-node topology for all cells.\n- Communication: all methods use the same node-local NVLink transport; no sharding/overlap knobs changed except what the schedule itself implies.\n- For 1F1B-I: c = 2 chunks fixed; for ZB-1p/ZB-2p: no interleaving/chunking.\n- Same process count per pipeline, same logging interval, same seeds across corresponding cells.\n\n## 5) Bubble-vs-overhead decomposition\nGoal: attribute observed TP changes to (a) bubble shrinkage (the phenomenon under study) vs (b) large-m overhead growth (more communication events and kernel launches per step).\n- Bubble estimate: if the harness (e.g., sail-sg/zero-bubble) exposes per-stage scheduler timestamps/idle counters, compute steady-state idle fraction per stage: bubble(M,m) = idle_time/(busy+idle) over the measurement window. Where timing counters are not exposed, fall back to an inferred bubble: run a non-pipelined proxy to estimate the per-microbatch compute time \u03c4 (e.g., single-GPU forward+backward of the same stage module on the same hardware with identical micro_batch), then the perfectly-packed ceiling is TP_ideal = (p\u00b7num_workers\u00b7tokens_per_microbatch)/\u03c4 and inferred bubble = 1 \u2212 TP/TP_ideal. This ceiling also serves as the normalization reference for all methods.\n- Communication accounting: instrument per-step counts of point-to-point messages (activations/gradients) and total bytes moved per stage per step under each (M, m). Report message_count(m) and comm_time(m) per method. Because fixed micro_batch keeps payloads constant, any TP decrease at large m that coincides with linearly/quadratically growing message counts and measured comm time is attributed to communication/kernel-launch overhead, NOT bubble.\n- Decision rule to separate effects: plot TP, bubble, and message_count all against m. If bubble(M,m) \u2192 ~0 while TP still rises, the remaining gap is overhead; if bubble(M,m) tracks the TP curve\u2019s rise, the rise is bubble-driven. Report both curves so the two can be distinguished rather than conflated.\n\n## 6) Replication and statistical plan (pre-registered)\n- Repetition: n = 3 independent seeds per (M, m) at primary config (p=4). Post-hoc rule: if the 95% CI half-width of normalized TP exceeds the plateau tolerance \u00b15%, add seeds until it is below \u00b15% or n = 5.\n- Normalized curves: for each method and seed, T_norm(M,m) = TP(M,m)/TP(M,m_max=64). Report mean \u00b1 95% CI (t-interval across seeds, or bootstrap if n small) vs m for all four methods, plus a table of absolute TP(M,m).\n- O1/O2 (\u201cimproves with m\u201d) decision: claim holds for method M if (i) mean TP(M,64) > mean TP(M, m_min_valid) with non-overlapping 95% CIs, and (ii) the rank correlation (Kendall \u03c4, \u03b1 = 0.05) between m and mean TP is positive across the swept levels. Report also the slope of TP vs log2 m as a magnitude for \u201chow much improve.\u201d\n- O3 (\u201cZB-2p retains high throughput at few microbatches\u201d) decision, pre-registered: passes if mean TP(ZB-2p, m=2) \u2265 0.95 \u00d7 mean TP(ZB-2p, m=64) AND the lower 95% CI bound of the ratio TP(ZB-2p, 2)/TP(ZB-2p, 64) \u2265 0.90. Same test applied at m=4 as a secondary check. If m=2 is illegal for ZB-2p, apply at the smallest legal m.\n- Cross-method contrast: report ZB-2p at small m vs every other method at the same m (CI-based comparison) to make the \u201cfewer microbatches\u201d advantage explicit.\n- Confirmatory/sensitivity: p=8 subset (4 methods \u00d7 {4,8,16,32} \u00d7 2 seeds) must reproduce the same qualitative ordering (ZB-2p flat; 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p rising) or explain the deviation.\n- Analysis is scripted (single CSV of (M, m, p, seed, TP, idle_frac, msg_count, comm_time), one analysis script) to enforce the pre-registered rules; no post-hoc redefinition of the plateau threshold.\n\n## 7) Feasibility\nHardware: requires at least 4 GPUs (A100/H100-class recommended for realistic LLM shapes); 8 GPUs recommended to allow the p=8 subset and parallel seed jobs. Single intra-node (NVLink) assumed; NCCL required.\nSoftware/dependencies: PyTorch \u2265 2.0 + NCCL; NVIDIA Apex (fused AdamW) \u2014 build from source; clone the sail-sg/zero-bubble Megatron fork and build per its README; flash-attention optional but must be constant across all cells if used; CUDA 12.x toolchain recommended. Note: no network, no GPU, and no dependencies exist in this sandbox, so installation must happen on the target cluster.\nRough runtime estimate (primary grid only): with hidden 1024 / 4 stages / micro_batch 4 / seq 2048 on a modern GPU, per-step \u2248 0.5\u20132 s. One run \u2248 warm-up (\u224820\u2013100 steps) + 100 measured steps \u2248 1\u20134 min \u2192 24 primary cells \u00d7 3 seeds = 72 runs \u2248 1.5\u20136 GPU-hours if seeds are parallelized across 4 GPUs. Adding the p=8 subset (32 runs \u2248 1\u20132 GPU-hours) and the optional fixed-global-batch sensitivity (12 runs) keeps the total on the order of 3\u201310 GPU-hours wall-clock with job parallelism \u2014 a small, tractable sweep. Exact times depend on model/GPU; the protocol is robust to slower hardware because N_measure is fixed in steps, not seconds.\nExplicit statement: this sandbox (no GPU, no network, empty repo, no installed packages) CANNOT execute this experiment. The design is a runnable specification for the target environment only; no experimental execution is performed or claimed in this deliverable.\n\n## 8) Consolidated assumptions and uncertainties\nAssumptions (carried as labels, not source-verified facts):\n- A1. Methods match the ZB paper (Qi et al., ICLR 2024) and sail-sg/zero-bubble semantics: 1F1B/1F1B-I are standard Megatron schedules (bubble \u2248 (p\u22121)/(p+m\u22121) and \u2248 (p\u22121)/(c\u00b7m+p\u22121)); ZB-1p = zero-bubble 1F1B steady state; ZB-2p = zero-bubble 2F2B steady state.\n- A2. Throughput = steady-state tokens/s over a window after warmup/before cooldown; tokens = N_measure \u00d7 m \u00d7 micro_batch \u00d7 seq_len.\n- A3. \u201cImproves with more microbatches\u201d = monotone rise of mean TP over the swept m (per O1/O2 rule); ZB-2p \u201cretains high throughput at few microbatches\u201d = the O3 rule (mean at smallest m \u2265 95% of own ceiling; CI lower bound \u2265 90%).\n- A4. Primary batch framing: micro_batch fixed = 4, global_batch = m \u00d7 micro_batch (so per-step tokens grow with m; token normalization equalizes the comparison).\n- A5. 1F1B-I uses c = 2 chunks, fixed; model layer count is divisible by p and p\u00b7c at both p levels.\n- A6. All cells share model, seq_len, optimizer, precision, comm backend, and seed handling; only M, m, (p) vary.\n- A7. The harness either exposes scheduler timing counters (allowing direct bubble/idle measurement) or can be complemented by an offline per-microbatch compute proxy for the inferred-bubble fallback.\n- A8. The design is a runnable specification; execution would occur on external multi-GPU hardware.\n\nUncertainties:\n- U1. Exact knob/CLI names, throughput-unit conventions, and warmup handling of the real harness are unknown (no repo present).\n- U2. Whether a given scheduler enforces a minimum m (e.g., m \u2265 p), which would force dropping small-m cells and adjusting the O3 test to the smallest legal m.\n- U3. The precise minimal-m condition for ZB-1p\u2019s zero-bubble regime \u2014 the design\u2019s wide m sweep (2\u219264) covers both possibilities (elbow at small or large m) without assuming one.\n- U4. Whether 1F1B-I and ZB variants share scheduler code paths in the real repo, affecting isolation purity; mitigated by reporting raw message/comm/idle counts per cell.\n- U5. Large-m overhead (communication + launch growth) can depress TP independent of bubble; mitigated by the decomposition in \u00a75 and the pre-registered dual-curve reporting.\n- U6. Run-to-run variance without GPU pinning; mitigated by \u22653 seeds, CI-based rules, and determinism settings.\n\nDeliverable integrity: no experiment was run; no execution is claimed; all literature-based statements about the methods are assumptions (A1, A5) rather than repository-verified facts.", "evidence": "Environment inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; embedded track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Filesystem-wide searches for pipeline/zero-bubble sources, .git dirs, and ML packages (torch/megatron/deepspeed/fairscale) found none; Python 3.14.4 with no project deps; no /dev/nvidia devices; no network (no resolv.conf); nproc=224. This confirms source-based summarization is impossible and validates the design-only, domain-grounded approach. Design content rests on the ZB-paper bubble formulas (1F1B \u2248 (p\u22121)/(p+m\u22121); interleaved \u2248 (p\u22121)/(cm+p\u22121)) and the ZB-1p/ZB-2p schedule semantics from Qi et al., ICLR 2024, all flagged as assumptions.", "assumptions": ["A1 Methods match the ZB paper (Qi et al., ICLR 2024) and sail-sg/zero-bubble semantics: 1F1B/1F1B-I standard Megatron schedules; ZB-1p = zero-bubble 1F1B steady state; ZB-2p = zero-bubble 2F2B steady state.", "A2 Throughput = steady-state tokens/s computed over a fixed measurement window after a stability-based warmup and before cooldown; tokens = N_measure x global_batch x seq_len.", "A3 'Improves with more microbatches' = monotone rise of mean TP over the swept m per the pre-registered O1/O2 rule; ZB-2p 'retains high throughput at few microbatches' = O3 rule (mean at smallest m >= 95% of its own ceiling, CI lower bound >= 90%).", "A4 Primary batch framing: micro_batch fixed at 4; global_batch = m x micro_batch; throughput token-normalized so growing per-step tokens do not bias comparisons.", "A5 1F1B-I uses c=2 chunks fixed; model layer count (32) divisible by p and p*c at p=4 and p=8.", "A6 All cells share model size, seq_len, optimizer, precision, communication backend, and seed handling; only M, m, (p) vary.", "A7 The harness either exposes scheduler timing/counters for direct bubble measurement or can be complemented by an offline per-microbatch compute proxy for an inferred-bubble fallback.", "A8 The design is a runnable specification for external multi-GPU hardware; this sandbox cannot and does not execute it."], "uncertainties": ["U1 Exact config-knob/CLI names, throughput units, and warmup handling of the real harness are unknown because no source is present in the workspace.", "U2 A scheduler may enforce a minimum m (e.g., m>=p), forcing small-m cells to be dropped and the O3 test to use the smallest legal m; the wide sweep partially hedges this.", "U3 The precise minimal-m condition for ZB-1p's zero-bubble regime is unknown; the sweep from m=2 to 64 covers both an early and a late elbow.", "U4 1F1B-I and ZB variants may share scheduler code paths in the real repo, limiting isolation purity; mitigated by reporting raw message/comm/idle counts per cell.", "U5 Large-m communication and kernel-launch overhead can depress TP independently of bubble; mitigated by the bubble-vs-overhead decomposition and dual-curve reporting.", "U6 Run-to-run variance on unshared hardware; mitigated by >=3 seeds, deterministic settings, and CI-based decision rules."]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
